Every single action should be wrapped with a detailed checksumAI description. This is very important and accomplished two major goals:
It provides a user friendly interface when reading reports



It allows the AI to better understand when a test fails and attempt to automatically heal a test based on the actions description. Without this, the AI and anybody unfamiliar with the test case from our team or from the customer will find it very difficult and frustrating to decipher what the action is supposed to be doing - especially when it fails!

Important! We wrap Actions only. We do not wrap assertions. For example:
DO NOT DO THE FOLLOWING:
await checksumAI("Expect Reject button to have spanish translation", () =>
     expect(
       page.getByLabel("Rechazar todo")
     ).toBeVisible({ timeout: 5000 })
   );



Descriptive Actions and Assertions
Describing Actions with checksumAI
The checksumAI method describes the action, and every action must include a description. Ensure the description is detailed enough so that someone only seeing the HTML and the description can understand what needs to be done. Avoid using data-specific information to prevent flakiness. Always include both the action and intent.
Always use meaningful descriptors when writing comments or naming actions in your code. This helps make your code easier to understand and maintain, especially when using checksumAI. Avoid vague terms like "click" or "go to" and instead provide specific context.
BAD:
await checksumAI('Go to', () =>
	...
}));
await checksumAI('Click dropdown', () =>
	...
);


GOOD:
await checksumAI('Navigate to the signup email page', () =>
	...
}));
	await checksumAI('Click on Country of Residence dropdown menu', () =>
	...
);

By combining thoughtful descriptors and clear assertion comments, your code becomes more maintainable and easier to troubleshoot.

Handling Loops and Dynamic Data
Working with Loops and Dynamic Data
When running actions in a for loop, you can either place the checksumAI inside the loop and make the description generic enough to accommodate multiple iterations or use dynamic data within the string to maintain accuracy.
Handling Variable Data
For applications where data is consistent (e.g., user created at the beginning of every test), it’s safe to use static selectors. However, if the data is likely to change, ensure selectors are not bound to changeable data elements.
Example: Avoid using a selector tied to a username or session ID that may vary between tests.

Using variableStore
Each test should contain a variableStore when using custom variables
test(defineChecksumTest("Delete a product", "G8RlA"), async ({ page, variablesStore }) => {


Use this instead of creating a variable with const or let
variablesStore.titleValue = "cktest-" + Date.now();


This removes variable scope and allows the use of variables to be more streamlined during test review. VariableStore can be accessed by using variableStore, variablesStore , or vs.

Time-Based and Unique Data
Handling Date and Time Selectors
If you need to use a selector related to a relative date (e.g., today’s date), create a function that returns the correct selector based on the current date.
Generating Unique Inputs
For inputs that need to be unique (e.g., when testing new form entries), use Date.now() to sgenerate a unique string. This ensures no overlap between test runs.
IMPORTANT! Use Checksum naming schema
For example:
`cktest-${Date.now()}`

Selectors and Best Practices
Avoid Flaky Selectors Flaky selectors can cause tests to fail unpredictably when minor changes are made to the page. Instead of selecting a parent element and filtering by text, it's more reliable to click the child element directly.
Bad Example: This uses a generic CSS class that might change, which could break your test.
.locator(".css-13b6tc8-control")

Good Example: This uses meaningful class names that are less likely to change.
.locator(".blotteroid")
.locator(".view-lines")

Avoid Generated or Dynamic CSS Classes Many frameworks generate CSS classes dynamically. These classes may change between builds, leading to test failures. It's better to avoid these and use selectors based on roles, labels, or more stable attributes.
Bad Example: Dynamic class names like the one below are unstable.
.locator(".css-13b6tc8-control")

Good Example: Using class names that are more stable and related to specific functionality.
.locator(".blotteroid")
.locator(".view-lines")

Avoid Overly Deep DOM Traversal Drilling too deep into the DOM can make selectors brittle because even small changes in the hierarchy will break your tests. Aim for concise and meaningful selectors.
Bad Example: This selector relies on the exact structure of the DOM and is prone to breaking if the layout changes.
.locator("div:nth-child(2) > .w-100 > .css-13b6tc8-control > .css-1wy0on6 > div")

Good Example: A more stable and readable approach that focuses on role and exact text match.
.locator("button")
.getByText("Run", { exact: true })

Use Role-Based Selectors Accessibility roles provide stable and meaningful selectors that are less likely to break, especially when the structure of the page changes.
Good Example: Selecting elements based on their roles ensures more reliable interactions.
.getByRole("option", { name: "LanceID1" })
.getByRole("checkbox")

Avoid Using .first(), .last(), or .nth() These methods are dependent on the order of elements in the DOM, which could change if new elements are added or if the structure is updated.
Bad Example: Relying on the position of elements in the DOM is risky because new elements may be added.
.locator('[aria-multiselectable="false"]').nth(2)
.locator("[id='react-select-8-option-2']").first()

Good Example: This selector uses role-based identification, which remains stable even if new elements are added.
.getByRole("img", { name: "add-file" })
.getByRole("option", { name: "TPCDS_SF100TCL", exact: true })

Prioritize Readable and Maintainable Selectors Always prioritize selectors that can be easily understood and maintained by others. Avoid complex chains that are difficult to follow or modify.
Good Example: Simple, role-based selectors are more maintainable.
.getByRole("button", { name: "Submit" })


Assertions and Best Practices
Adding Strong Assertions
Avoid using assertions that pass based on text alone, especially since the .toHaveText() assertion includes child elements, which could lead to false positives if unexpected text is nested inside. It's better to assert based on the visibility or the role of elements to make the test more robust and resilient to changes in structure or content.
Bad Example:
await expect(page.getByText("Options list")).toHaveText("Options list");

This example could fail if any child element inside the target includes or interferes with the text.
Good Example:
await expect(page.getByText("Options list")).toBeVisible();

Here, the test is more robust, focusing on visibility, which is more likely to represent the actual state of the page.

Using Variables to Store Data for Later Assertions
In dynamic tests, it's helpful to store values, like prices or item IDs, in variables during the test. These variables can be used for subsequent assertions, allowing you to create more meaningful and precise checks. This ensures that your selectors are reliable and the test remains consistent even with different data or DOM changes.
Scenario Walkthrough:
Wait for a Valid Value: The test ensures that the currentPipValue contains a valid numerical value by checking for NaN and waiting if necessary.
while (isNaN(parseFloat(currentPipValue))) { 
	await page.waitForTimeout(500); 
	currentPipValue = await priceBoardPip.innerText(); 
}

Interaction and Selection: The test interacts with the page, selecting a new value from the dropdown based on the updated Pip price, ensuring the new selection is higher.


await checksumAI("Click on Bid dropdown for first currency pair", () => 
	page
	.locator(".qomain > div")
	.filter({ hasText: "Bid" })
	.first()
	.click());
	const newPipValueText 
		= (parseFloat(currentPipValue) + 2).toString();

Assert with Stored Data: Finally, it asserts that the new Pip value selected is greater than the original:


expect(
	parseFloat(newPipValueText), 
"The selected price should be greater than the current pip value"
).toBeGreaterThan(parseFloat(currentPipValue));


Later, you can use the stored variables to make your selectors more robust:
await checksumAI("Click on order id", () =>
	page.checksumSelector("3TRyZ")
	.getByRole("link", { name: `${orderId}`, exact: true }).click()
);

By storing key variables like the orderId, you ensure the selector targets the correct element dynamically, even in scenarios where page content may change.
Including REGEX
When dealing with dynamic elements or content-driven pages, always strive to make your assertions robust. This means considering factors like visibility, roles, and even regex patterns for more complex text matches. The more robust your assertion, the less likely it is to break with minor UI changes.
Example:
await expect(
	page.locator(".orderticket row")
	.filter({ hasText: "Limit Rate" }), 
	"The limit rate should match the selected price"
).toHaveText(new RegExp("\\d+\\.\\d{2}${newPipValueText}\\d*"));

In this case, a regular expression ensures that the expected value dynamically matches the correct format and precision.

Additional Notes
CSS Animations and Network Requests: Keep in mind that CSS animations and selectors are faster than network requests. Even if a button appears active or a spinner has stopped, the test could still fail if the page is waiting on a network response. Always use more reliable assertions like toBeVisible() and avoid relying purely on visual states.

Timeouts and Efficient Waits
Managing Timeouts and Race Conditions
Race conditions can cause tests to behave inconsistently or fail intermittently. To avoid these issues, it’s crucial to handle waiting effectively without relying on arbitrary timeouts, which may not guarantee that the page is ready.
Avoiding Fixed Timeouts
Using fixed page.waitForTimeout() values may seem like a quick solution to handle waits, but it leads to unreliable tests. This approach assumes a fixed time for an action to complete, which may vary depending on the environment or server response. If the time isn’t long enough, the test will fail; if it’s too long, the test wastes valuable time. Instead, rely on built-in waiting mechanisms, which automatically account for page readiness.
Example of Bad Use of Fixed Timeouts:
await page.waitForTimeout(3_000);
if ((await page.getByText("Bamboo HR").count()) > 0) {     
	await deleteCard(page, "Bamboo HR"); 
}
await page.waitForTimeout(3_000);

In this example, the test is made fragile by forcing arbitrary pauses before and after actions. This can result in failures when the page loads slower than expected or causes unnecessary delays.
Using Built-In Timeouts for Actions and Assertions
Playwright provides built-in timeouts for actions such as clicking buttons, interacting with elements, or performing assertions. These timeouts will automatically wait for the required conditions to be met, improving test reliability and reducing the risk of race conditions.
Good Example with Action Timeouts:
if ((await page.getByText("Bamboo HR").count()) > 0) {    
	 await deleteCard(page, "Bamboo HR"); 
}
await checksumAI("Click on the Add System button", () =>     
	page.getByText("Add System").click()
);

This approach ensures that Playwright waits for the “Add System” button to be clickable within the specified timeout period.
Example of Assertion Timeouts:
await expect(page.getByText("Data system deleted"),     
"A confirmation message indicating the system was deleted should appear"
).toBeVisible({ timeout: 60_000 });

Here, the test waits up to 60 seconds for the confirmation message to appear. Using built-in assertion timeouts ensures the test passes only when the expected condition is met within the given timeframe, without relying on arbitrary pauses.
Efficient Waiting Techniques
DO NOT USE!!: waitForSelector(): This is not a web first assertion, so we avoid it. Use an expect statement instead to assert for visibility :(
Bad:
await page.waitForSelector('.submit-button', { timeout: 5_000 });

Good:
await expect(
     page.locator(".submit-button"),
     "Verify the Submit button is visible"
   ).toBeVisible();

DO NOT USE!!: waitForNavigation():
await page.click('a#navigate-button');
await page.waitForNavigation({ waitUntil: 'networkidle' });

waitForEvent(): Listen for specific events like downloading a file or opening a new tab.
const downloadPromise = page.waitForEvent('download');
await page.click('.download-link');
await downloadPromise;

When Adjusting Timeouts is Necessary:
Built-in timeouts should cover most cases, but there are situations where you may need to adjust them. For example:
Large datasets may take longer to load.
Network latency in different environments can vary.
Complex DOM structures may require additional processing time.
In these cases, it’s acceptable to extend the timeout but keep it reasonable to avoid unnecessarily long waits.
Summary:
Avoid waitForTimeout() unless absolutely necessary.
Use Playwright’s built-in waiting mechanisms for actions and assertions.
Adjust timeouts thoughtfully when needed to handle specific conditions.
This approach keeps the codebase clean, reduces flakiness, and improves test reliability.

