Ensuring a Clean Slate Before Each Test
It's crucial to start each test with the application in a clean, predictable state to avoid data pollution and unintended side effects across test runs. Rather than focusing solely on returning the application to its original state at the end of the test, it’s often more effective to check and reset the state before each test begins. This ensures that every test runs in isolation, unaffected by the results or failures of previous tests.
Considerations for Starting Tests:
Pre-Test State Validation and Cleanup:
Before executing test logic, use a try/catch block to validate the current state of the application. If the application isn’t in a "blank slate" state (e.g., there are leftover items, data, or configurations from previous tests), reset the state within the try block, ensuring that each test starts fresh.
Here’s an example where we are selecting a dropdown and clicking “Resume”, because the state of the test relies on the “Pause” option to be available which is only present after resuming.
try {
     await page
       .locator('[role="row"]')
       .locator('[aria-colindex="9"]')
       .locator(".ant-space-item")
       .click();
     await page.getByText(/Resume/i).click();
   } catch {
     console.log("Job run is already active");
   }


A Try/Catch is just one tool, but isn’t always necessary:
For example, the site may have built in functionality for resetting data. We can leverage this to call a function before each test run to ensure the state is clean.
export const clearAllOrders = async (page: Page) => {
  try {
    await page.locator("[name='offAllOrders']").first().click({ timeout: 5_000 });
    await page.getByRole("button", { name: "YES" }).click();
    await page.getByRole("button", { name: "CLOSE", exact: true }).click({ timeout: 5_000 });
  } catch {
    console.log("No orders to clear in Order Status");
  }
};


QA Engineer’s Responsibility:
While automated tests can ensure much of the setup and cleanup, it’s still the QA engineer’s responsibility to review whether further custom logic is required to return the application to a known state before running the next test. This helps avoid test flakiness and ensures each test is executed in a clean, reliable environment.
By using this pre-test approach, tests remain isolated, ensuring a higher level of reliability and maintainability, even if previous tests failed or left behind residual data.


