Use Web First Assertions
Assertions are used to verify if the expected result matches the actual result. By using web first assertions in Playwright, the framework waits until the expected condition is met before proceeding. This is useful when, for example, you're testing an alert message that appears after a button click. If the alert message takes half a second to appear, using an assertion like toBeVisible() will make Playwright wait and retry until the condition is satisfied.
Example (Correct Usage):
await expect(page.getByText('welcome')).toBeVisible();


Example (Incorrect Usage):

await expect(
    page.getByText('welcome').isVisible()
).toBe(true);


URL Assertions
In URL testing scenarios, web first assertions also help by waiting for the URL to match the expected value before moving forward.
Example (Correct Usage):
await expect(page, 
    "The URL should be https://app.deriv.com/"
).toHaveURL("https://app.deriv.com/");


Example (Incorrect Usage):

await expect(page.url(), 
    "The URL should be https://app.deriv.com/"
).toBe( "https://app.customer.com/");



Read more about web first assertions here

Use of expect.poll
How Playwright Polling Works

The .poll() method repeatedly checks a condition until it either passes or times out. Here's how this specific poll works:

await expect
  .poll(
    // Callback function that returns a value to check
    async () => await page.locator('[class*="circular-loader"]').count(),
    // Options object
    { timeout: TIMEOUTS.medium }
  )
  // Expected value we're waiting for
  .toBe(0);


Breaking it down:
1. The callback function counts how many elements with class containing "circular-loader" are present on the page
2. This check is repeated periodically (by default every 500ms)
3. The polling continues until either:
   - The count equals 0 (success case)
   - The timeout is reached (failure case)

In this test, it's being used to wait for loading spinners to disappear from the page. The test will only proceed once all circular loaders are gone (count = 0), or it will fail if the timeout is reached before that happens.

Why Polling is Better

This is more reliable than a simple wait because:
- It's dynamic - completes as soon as the condition is met
- It handles variable loading times
- It confirms the loading actually completed rather than just waiting a fixed time

The equivalent without polling might look like:

// Less reliable approach
await page.waitForTimeout(2000); // Arbitrary wait that might be too short or too long

// Another approach, but only works for visibility
await expect(page.locator('[class*="circular-loader"]')).toBeHidden();



Working with Multiple Tabs
To manage multi-tab test flows in Checksum, you can use the getPage() method. This method retrieves the ChecksumPage based on the order of tab appearance, with the first tab (initially opened) indexed as 0.
getPage(index: number): Promise<ChecksumPage>;


Example Usage
Clicking a Button on the Customer Website
Execute an action on the customer website, such as clicking a button, using checksumAI().
await checksumAI("Click on View my requests", () => 
  page
    .getByRole("button", { name: "View Requests" })
    .click() );


Waiting for the New Page to Load
Wait for the new page or tab to load after the interaction.

