Tips for Speeding up Test edits
When reviewing a large test case it is important to comment out code that has already been tested, if commenting out will not impact the rest of the test.

Wait for Network Responses, not timeouts
Playwright doc here

It is best practice to avoid using page.waitForTimeout whenever possible to avoid flaky results. Instead, after performing an action, for example click(), it’s best practice to view the network tab and look for the network query being triggered and verify that the response finishes before proceeding with the test.

In this example we use regex to grab a param of “_query” from the address and wait for that query to finish before proceeding with the test. (this is an actual example from an existing test)

const responsePromise = page.waitForResponse(/_query/); 
await page.getByText("download'').click(); 
await responsePromise;


Using Page Object Model (POM)
Playwright doc here

The Playwright documentation on Page Object Models (POM) highlights how this approach can improve test authoring and maintenance. A page object represents a section of your application, encapsulating element locators and reusable actions for that page. For example, a typical e-commerce app might define separate models for the homepage, product listings, and checkout.
By centralizing locators and logic in page objects, test code becomes cleaner and easier to maintain. When elements change, updates are limited to the relevant page object, avoiding widespread refactoring. POMs also promote reusability, reducing duplication in your test suite.

Using External or Added Files in Tests
For tests requiring file uploads, this method resolves to the configured assets folder storing all relevant files
For example, a test needs to upload a test.pdf file on a customer's website.
Store the file inside /checksum/test-data/bin
Call page.resolveAssetsFolder(['test.pdf']) within your codebase to retrieve the file path

