Creating an API
Testing frequently relies on utilizing the web app’s user interface to perform certain actions. Not every test requires testing the UI of an action, but may rely on the action for additional testing requirements. For example, a customer may have a feature to create a new blueprint. One test will certainly need to be in place that clicks through the user interface to ensure blueprints can be created. Other tests however, that utilize the blueprint functionality, don’t need to test the UI but rather simply require a blueprint exist for use. In these cases it is best practice to create an API that can be called to create a blueprint and bypass the user interface for speed and ease throughout the rest of the testing suite.
