Using Test.step
It’s best practice to wrap test code with test.step so that repetitive actions and assertions make better sense when trying to understand the playwright's report.

Watch this video to better understand this concept which further branches into the next section.

Example:


await test.step("Verify all checkboxes are checked at the same time", async () => {
     if (webOnly) {
       await expect(
         page
           .locator('[data-testid="summary-time-series"]')
           .locator('.check_box--label:has-text("Web Only")')
       ).toHaveCSS("background-color", "rgb(0, 117, 201)", { timeout: 3_000 });
     }
     if (organic) {
       await expect(
         page
           .locator('[data-testid="summary-time-series"]')
           .locator('.check_box--label:has-text("Organic")')
       ).toHaveCSS("background-color", "rgb(0, 117, 201)", { timeout: 3_000 });
     }

     if (unique) {
       await expect(
         page
           .locator('[data-testid="summary-time-series"]')
           .locator('.check_box--label:has-text("Unique")')
       ).toHaveCSS("background-color", "rgb(0, 117, 201)", { timeout: 3_000 });
     }
});



Skipping steps mid-test
Sometimes it is necessary to skip only a portion or single part of a test due to a known bug, functionality may be temporarily unavailable or turned off, or other reasons specific to the customer's web app environment. One way to work around this would be to simply comment out code, but this method is not pleasant to look at and can be confused as dated code that should actually be removed.

Playwright has built in methods to skip parts of tests that also include a visible comment:

   …
   await checksumAI("Click on Back to Question", () =>
     page.getByTestId("go-back-to-question").click()
   );

   await questionStudio.verifyDateDifference(
     variableStore.calcKey,
     "days",
     "dateTime",
     "dateTime2"
   );

   await checksumAI("Click on Back", () =>
     page.getByRole("link", { name: "Back" }).click()
   );

   // START SKIP HERE
   await test.step("Verify date formatting and calculation", async (step) => {
     step.skip(true, "Date format verification is temporarily disabled");

     await questionStudio.updateDateFormat("dateTime", 0);
     await questionStudio.updateDateFormat("dateTime2", 0);

     await questionStudio.verifyDateDifference(
       variableStore.calcKey,
       "days",
       "dateTime",
       "dateTime2"
     );
   });


We prioritize this method over commenting out code since it’s easy to read and shows up as a step during the test run so that customers can decipher and be reminded of the skip when reviewing their daily reports.

